WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.033
Random variables.

2
00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:06.067
This is how real world randomness becomes math we can work with.

3
00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:13.067
A random variable is a variable whose value is the numeric outcome of a random process.

4
00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:18.233
Notice that it is the mapping from outcomes to numbers, not any one particular number.

5
00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:21.267
Here is the simplest example.

6
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:22.554
Flip four coins.

7
00:00:22.554 --> 00:00:25.700
The outcome is a messy sequence of heads and tails.

8
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.035
Count the heads.

9
00:00:27.035 --> 00:00:29.300
That rule is the random variable X.

10
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.967
Now every outcome becomes a number from zero to four.

11
00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:37.800
Once something is a random variable, it gets a distribution.

12
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.143
Two heads is the most likely, at thirty seven point five percent.

13
00:00:42.143 --> 00:00:43.200
All four is rare.

14
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.633
Random variables come in two flavors.

15
00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:49.346
Discrete ones have countable outcomes.

16
00:00:49.346 --> 00:00:52.267
Continuous ones can take any value in a range.

17
00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:55.119
Dice rolls and event counts are discrete.

18
00:00:55.119 --> 00:00:58.667
Heights, temperatures, and noise voltages are continuous.

19
00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:02.200
Either way, they get a mean, a variance, and a distribution.

20
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.867
Let's sample one in code.

21
00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:10.633
We simulate ten thousand ten day windows, where each day has a fifty percent chance of a flare.

22
00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:13.000
X is the number of flares per window.

23
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:18.167
Counting how often each value appears gives X's empirical distribution.

24
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:20.700
Here is a real use.

25
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.033
Your agent summarizes near earth asteroid close approaches per week.

26
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:27.608
Next week's count is a random variable.

27
00:01:27.608 --> 00:01:31.167
You do not know it, but you can characterize it from history.

28
00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:36.467
That lets the agent say twelve would be unusually high, instead of printing a bare number.

29
00:01:37.667 --> 00:01:38.474
Let's recap.

30
00:01:38.474 --> 00:01:41.367
A random variable maps outcomes to numbers.

31
00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:43.667
It can be discrete or continuous.

32
00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:46.933
And it earns a distribution, a mean, and a variance.

33
00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:49.600
Next up: probability distributions.
